+\subsection{Mapping rank across thinking modes}
+\label{app:cot-rank}
+
+We distinguish differences across thinking modes from changes within a reasoning
+trajectory. The cross-mode comparison maps the last prompt-token state with
+thinking off to that mode's own answer, and the end-of-CoT state with thinking on
+to its own answer. Both the input state and answer target therefore change.
+The same-answer comparison instead maps the thinking-on prompt and end-of-CoT
+states to identical thinking-on answer targets, as in the main CoT comparison.
+All maps in this analysis use Qwen3-8B at layer 24, with 4,096-dimensional inputs
+and answer-token-mean targets.
+
+\paragraph{Predictive rank.}
+We summarize a fitted affine map by the number of output directions needed to
+approximate its full-rank prediction accuracy. We order directions by PCA of
+the map's fitted outputs on the training questions. Let $P_r$ project onto the
+first $r$ directions, and retain the training-answer mean $\mu_A$ when truncating:
+$f_r(x)=\mu_A+P_r(f(x)-\mu_A)$. We define
+\[
+ r_{\epsilon}=\min\left\{r\in\{0,\ldots,4096\}:
+ \operatorname{MSE}_{\mathrm{val}}(f_r)
+ \leq (1+\epsilon)\operatorname{MSE}_{\mathrm{val}}(f)\right\}.
+\]
+Our primary summary is $r_{0.10}$: the smallest rank in this nested truncation
+sequence whose validation squared error is at most 10\% above the full map's.
+This is not a rule retaining 90\% of $R^2$. The PCA directions use training data,
+the rank uses validation data, and predictive performance is evaluated on test
+data. We also report $\epsilon=0.05$ and $0.20$, without additional map fits.
+
+\paragraph{Data and fitting.}
+The general-chat comparison uses banked, model-generated answers to
+LMSYS-Chat-1M prompts \citep{zheng2024lmsyschat}, with one answer per prompt and
+mode. The thinking-off and thinking-on maps retain 9,937 and 9,968 training
+pairs, respectively, 399 validation pairs each, and 998 and 995 test pairs.
+Both use the original ridge penalty of 1000 and the original fixed split.
+The benchmark comparisons use the seven corpora and correctness labels in
+\appref{app:cot-details}: 4,522 questions correct only with thinking and 17,693
+correct in both modes. Maps and PCA bases are fit using all training questions,
+not only the scored subset. For each of the five outer folds, rank is selected
+on the subset's validation questions using three training folds; the map and
+basis are then refit on the four non-test folds before truncation at that rank. Input
+standardization and target centering use training data. Penalties remain 1000
+for prompt maps and 316.23 for the end-of-CoT map; only rank selection is nested.
+
