+
+\paragraph{Results and threshold sensitivity.}
+At the primary tolerance, cross-mode predictive rank falls from 122 to 74 on
+general chat but rises from 57 to 81 on questions correct only with thinking
+and from 72 to 92 on questions correct in both modes. Holding the benchmark
+answer targets fixed also gives higher rank at the end of CoT
+(\tabref{tab:cot-rank-thresholds}). Absolute ranks depend substantially on the
+tolerance, but these directions hold at each of the three tolerances tested.
+This check does not establish invariance to every possible cutoff.
+
+\paragraph{Predictive accuracy and scope.}
+Rank is relative to each map's own full-map error. Full-map test $R^2$ on chat
+is 0.689 for thinking-off prompt states and 0.657 for thinking-on end-of-CoT
+states at the matched layer. On the correct-only-with-thinking subset, the
+thinking-off prompt, thinking-on prompt and end-of-CoT scores are 0.433, 0.465
+and 0.535; on the both-correct subset they are 0.491, 0.499 and 0.559.
+Chat $R^2$ uses held-out target-mean centering. Benchmark scores here pool squared
+errors across questions and use training-fold within-corpus answer means;
+they are not the equal-corpus averages in \appref{app:cot-necessity}.
+The tolerance is not a common absolute $R^2$ target or a guarantee of preserving
+answer retrieval. Different generated targets, retained rows, input
+distributions and fitting recipes remain, and no sampling interval is supplied
+for the matched-layer chat rank. The original chat split also contains 13 exact
+prompt strings shared between validation and test, with none shared with training.
+These results describe predictive truncation
+rank, not an intrinsic dimension of reasoning or a causal change in the mapping
+operator.
+
+\paragraph{Copy baseline and answer retrieval.}
+On chat, identity plus a learned training-set bias gives $R^2=-1.097$ and
+$-0.167$ for thinking-off prompt and thinking-on end-of-CoT states. The full
+maps retrieve their own answers at 73.25\% and 69.65\% by raw cosine, among
+998 and 995 candidates (chance 0.1002\% and 0.1005\%). On benchmarks, in the
+same three-state order as above, identity-plus-bias $R^2$ is
+$(-2.341,-3.353,-1.095)$ for correct-only-with-thinking questions and
+$(-2.775,-3.552,-1.207)$ for both-correct questions. Full-map retrieval is
+$(90.11,87.95,98.76)\%$ and $(92.54,90.45,98.97)\%$, respectively, using
+training-fold whitening and CSLS with 6,762 candidates (chance 0.0148\%).
+Only benchmark queries are subset-filtered; candidate pools remain unchanged.
+Retrieval protocols differ between chat and benchmarks, so their absolute
+retrieval scores are not directly comparable.
